WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.765
LlamaIndex releases

00:00:01.576 --> 00:00:03.250
OCRia-nano-1

00:00:03.345 --> 00:00:04.206
an 8 .

00:00:04.017 --> 00:00:06.140
1M-parameter open-weight

00:00:05.999 --> 00:00:07.118
OCR model

00:00:07.638 --> 00:00:09.984
OCRia-nano-1 bundles

00:00:09.808 --> 00:00:11.046
seven tiny document

00:00:10.857 --> 00:00:12.260
models into LiteParse

00:00:12.072 --> 00:00:12.944
and parses a

00:00:12.756 --> 00:00:13.793
page in about

00:00:13.605 --> 00:00:16.399
426 ms on one

00:00:16.211 --> 00:00:17.201
CPU core

00:00:17.379 --> 00:00:18.640
with no GPU

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.208
and LlamaIndex says

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.116
it beats Marker

00:00:21.011 --> 00:00:23.295
PP-OCRv6 tiny and

00:00:23.107 --> 00:00:24.439
Tesseract on tables

00:00:24.251 --> 00:00:24.981
and layout

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:26.866
LlamaIndex reports

00:00:26.666 --> 00:00:28.576
59.2 overall on

00:00:28.387 --> 00:00:30.462
its ParseBench-2 benchmark

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:32.385
and 27.8 pages

00:00:32.196 --> 00:00:33.175
per second with

00:00:32.986 --> 00:00:34.613
16 single-core workers

00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:35.722
about twice the

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:37.007
throughput of LiteParse's

00:00:36.819 --> 00:00:38.410
default Tesseract setup

00:00:38.942 --> 00:00:39.779
The weights use

00:00:39.602 --> 00:00:40.828
a modified Open

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.807
RAIL-M license

00:00:41.914 --> 00:00:43.199
LiteParse itself is

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.826
Apache 2.0

00:00:45.366 --> 00:00:46.380
Full story on

00:00:46.191 --> 00:00:47.087
the dashboard
